::: callout-note
## Who is missing from Table 2?

A table split by income can only include people whose income is known, so the
87,423 respondents without an income
answer are left out of every column except "Overall". The same happens in any analysis that
drops incomplete answers ("complete-case analysis"). The rest of this chapter asks whether
that matters.
:::

## Testing a continuous variable {#sec-continuous-test}

**Does mean age differ across the four income groups?** `age` is the numeric
BRFSS variable `_AGE80`: CDC-imputed age in years, with everyone aged 80 or older
recorded as 80. The comparison therefore concerns this capped age variable,
not exact age above 80. See the
[CDC calculated-variable definitions](https://www.cdc.gov/brfss/annual_data/2024/pdf/2024-calculated-variables-version4-508.pdf).
The regression chapters continue to use `age_group`.

First select respondents with known income **from the full survey design**.
Report weighted means and 95% confidence intervals before interpreting a test.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
income_design <- design |> dplyr::filter(!is.na(income))
```
:::



::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
mean_age <- income_design |>
  dplyr::group_by(income) |>
  dplyr::summarise(
    age = srvyr::survey_mean(age, na.rm = TRUE, vartype = "ci")
  )
mean_age
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
# A tibble: 4 × 4
  income               age age_low age_upp
  <fct>              <dbl>   <dbl>   <dbl>
1 Less than $25,000   48.8    48.5    49.2
2 $25,000 to $49,999  49.0    48.7    49.3
3 $50,000 to $99,999  48.5    48.3    48.8
4 $100,000 or more    47.0    46.8    47.2
```


:::
:::


For more than two groups, fit a survey-weighted linear model and test the
income coefficients together. With four groups there are three coefficients;
the null hypothesis is that all three are zero, so all four population means
are equal. The joint Wald F test uses the survey design for its variance.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
age_fit <- survey::svyglm(age ~ income, design = income_design)
age_test <- survey::regTermTest(age_fit, ~income, method = "Wald")
rm(age_fit) # Cache the test result rather than the full model.
age_test
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
Wald test for income
 in svyglm(formula = age ~ income, design = income_design)
F =  65.4856  on  3  and  367882  df: p= < 2.22e-16 
```


:::
:::


The p-value is <0.001.
A small p-value is evidence that at least one mean differs; it does not identify
which pairs differ or establish that a difference is practically important.
Use the means and confidence intervals to describe the size of the differences.

### Put the mean test in a summary table

`gtsummary::add_p()` accepts a short custom function returning a p-value. This
function applies the same model and joint test to a numeric variable and a
categorical grouping variable.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
mean_test <- function(data, variable, by, ...) {
  fit <- survey::svyglm(stats::reformulate(by, variable), design = data)
  test <- survey::regTermTest(fit, stats::reformulate(by), method = "Wald")
  tibble::tibble(
    p.value = as.numeric(test$p),
    method = "Survey-weighted joint Wald test of means"
  )
}
```
:::



::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
age_table <- income_design |>
  gtsummary::tbl_svysummary(
    by = income,
    include = age,
    label = age ~ "Age (years, capped at 80)",
    type = age ~ "continuous",
    statistic = age ~ "{mean} ({sd})"
  ) |>
  gtsummary::add_p(test = age ~ mean_test)
age_table |>
  gtsummary::modify_caption("**Mean age (SD) by household income**")
```

