The parentheses contain the weighted **standard deviation**, which describes
variation in age, rather than the standard error of the mean. In a table with
both variable types, use `all_continuous() ~ mean_test` and
`all_categorical() ~ "svy.chisq.test"` in the `test` list.

For **two groups**, `survey::svyttest()` is a direct test of the difference in
means; the corresponding `gtsummary` alias is `"svy.t.test"`. The alias
`"svy.wald.test"` instead calls a contingency-table test of independence and
should not be used to test continuous means. See the
[gtsummary test definitions](https://www.danieldsjoberg.com/gtsummary/reference/tests.html).
Ordinary `t.test()` and `aov()` do not account for this survey design.

## Prevalence with confidence intervals

`srvyr::survey_mean()` estimates a weighted mean, here the share of adults with diabetes and
with fair or poor health, with its 95% confidence interval. Grouping by income keeps the
missing-income group as its own row.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
prevalence <- design |>
  dplyr::group_by(income) |>
  dplyr::summarise(
    diabetes = srvyr::survey_mean(diabetes_yes, na.rm = TRUE, vartype = "ci"),
    fair_poor = srvyr::survey_mean(fair_poor, na.rm = TRUE, vartype = "ci")
  )
prevalence
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

